#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161729 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a9a1aa0-8822-5dbd-8534-254ace55c555" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Canonical Form of a Quadratic Function by Finding Eigenvalues"}] :question/id #uuid "ce37b130-8aa9-58af-b3b8-bf78b5a741e7" :question/math-academy-id "e-8249" :question/problem "Using the method of orthogonal transformations, find the canonical form of $Q(\\mathbf{x})=x_1^2+x_2^2+2x_3^2+4x_1x_2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/assets/44753acffd8b6384cd291f9b0758edd7676acb319caa22d5b07be36dcf72a5a6.png)\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-4) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,2,\\,-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, writing the eigenvalues in descending order, we obtain the diagonal matrix\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/assets/217b5d7d3b82800bdfc44d83ca8b068e02a37759e52b7a8382505bfb102f753f.png)\n\nThe corresponding quadratic form after a change of variables is\n\n$$\n3y_1^2 + 2y_2^2 - y_3^2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161730 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a1bee282-2775-59f5-a929-9dc5327703bc" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Quadratic Function Over the 2D-Space to the Canonical Form Using Orthogonal Transformations"}] :question/id #uuid "c8ec4ba0-e0bf-5842-8f5e-4914ce46706a" :question/math-academy-id "e-8250" :question/problem "Given the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})=2x_1^2-2x_1x_2+2x_2^2,$ find its canonical (diagonal) form $\\mathbf{y}^T \\! D \\mathbf{y}$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $\\mathbf{x}=P\\mathbf{y}$ defines the corresponding change of variables." :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/assets/69dcf821f3ff202b567b901d8f512c5cecf7159617ca51a0664c14a5099e9715.png)\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +3 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For $\\lambda_1=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-3I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/assets/fc9465d4e5bcd43a3c29e5946b05ec18cdbc7453a4011aa548f374016c561b45.png) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_1$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_1  \\| = \\sqrt{2},$ we get \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/assets/59c1bd46325fab8a52fed162d7ae47e8fb33a10cc524cd770a3b18f5934e51dd.png)\n\n- For $\\lambda_2=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/assets/adc9241219f3b5ebce0d8418bf34e250b7230ea612fabca5bdfc109c98a18dbb.png) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_2$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_2  \\| = \\sqrt{2},$ we get \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/assets/dcab437eb77b29ab5bcd5c3ae14b77f10f01382aafadf2aecded1bb1fb72dc32.png)\n\nFinally, we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ (writing the eigenvalues in descending order) and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/assets/3f6b1002893290dde6c9a68425b0035e249ff3630a41d29c9da5756c1a18f6a6.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161731 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a07cbbfc-0f61-55e8-afe9-b675dc74f24e" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Quadratic Function Over the 3D-Space to the Canonical Form Using Orthogonal Transformations"}] :question/id #uuid "243d1a75-6f0f-5d55-a7ec-333487fa37bb" :question/math-academy-id "e-8251" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/assets/6135fe578745ebf2eccb4370557f0dba6ec27361a192e4e87536eadad87a39bf.png)\n\nConsider the matrices given above.\nFind an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $\\mathbf{x}=P\\mathbf{y}$ defines the corresponding change of variables that reduces the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x}$ to the canonical (diagonal) form $\\mathbf{y}^T \\! D \\mathbf{y}.$" :question/worked-solution "Every quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are \n\n$\\qquad$ $\\lambda_1=4, \\quad$ $\\lambda_2=2, \\quad$ and $\\quad\\lambda_3=1.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For $\\lambda_1=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-4I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/assets/13eee082d48c56e6c7e796549eddc0185868b39841a7c5946ce9ce9e996ba553.png) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_1$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_1  \\| = \\sqrt{2},$ we get \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/assets/1e7c81e373377bd719e1bbc9e87a8c88d13f6116bcf2949897fc26246e2265d1.png)\n\n- For $\\lambda_2=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/assets/61311029da90b54253a552ce2198ce9193d7f343c3ab345eea25419444da00a3.png) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_2$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_2  \\| = \\sqrt{2},$ we get \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/assets/3bf5334469b8b135548dfbd0c35725ee6927e2a169c6db37a1b0bae3f62695e5.png)\n\n- For $\\lambda_3=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/assets/1cb1b34b0df191e9fa63a907ba59e26a2ad16f3977e97d5b8588dc5e51f2c3a0.png) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_3$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_3  \\| = 1,$ we get \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/assets/39bf82ed6be834f41140a70e048268b39d47d7a0944461247d312d4efe95d184.png)\n\nFinally, we construct the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/assets/fb5604c6dc892a34af523d6b9b769cd50c59e82ed65d7be47cb89ed6044125bf.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382845 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a9a1aa0-8822-5dbd-8534-254ace55c555" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Canonical Form of a Quadratic Function by Finding Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc4a2702-aa5d-5fb1-b0fe-3b79eb0d05ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{y}_{1}^{2}+2{y}_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382847} {:answer/id #uuid "6fc0633a-7638-5a41-bdb3-d7441141c435" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{1}^{2}+2{y}_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382848} {:answer/id #uuid "4c0572aa-52b0-5bbb-a876-9df50af5e65a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382849} {:answer/id #uuid "90dea696-81e9-5ef7-9fbf-4f7bef5df96f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382850} {:answer/id #uuid "45dada6c-7f87-5235-83bd-93d961646b03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{1}^{2}-4{y}_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382851}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c0572aa-52b0-5bbb-a876-9df50af5e65a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382849} :answer-field/id #uuid "42b396d5-8731-5330-92fa-4be67c53c532" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382846}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7b1133d5-5872-572e-8342-de6cddf5444d" :question/math-academy-id "q-140987" :question/problem "Using the method of orthogonal transformations, find the canonical form of $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-9 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -8 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)(\\lambda -4) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =4,\\,-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, writing the eigenvalues in descending order, we obtain the diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe corresponding quadratic form after a change of variables is\n\n$$\n4{y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{2}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382852 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a1bee282-2775-59f5-a929-9dc5327703bc" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Quadratic Function Over the 2D-Space to the Canonical Form Using Orthogonal Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd1f2e33-eb57-5e09-b215-e048e45c6cfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382854} {:answer/id #uuid "dd20683e-c621-5ce2-8614-5bb5813ae2a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382855} {:answer/id #uuid "d1641326-3a1d-5f82-b577-063020e607d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382856} {:answer/id #uuid "1bd1d5fa-2f8e-5b7d-b369-fe53c27e995b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382857} {:answer/id #uuid "fdf59f1d-ae0b-55af-a225-2271d6cec7c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382858}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1641326-3a1d-5f82-b577-063020e607d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382856} :answer-field/id #uuid "45eb3de3-d29a-56c9-8763-211b7ec9c628" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382853}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2172cdc2-fb35-5393-84ba-3c3dbc3c7926" :question/math-academy-id "q-140997" :question/problem "Given the quadratic form $Q(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+5{x}_{2}^{2},$ find its canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables." :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda +6 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =6,\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-6I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]-6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-6I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-1I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]-1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-1I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ (writing the eigenvalues in descending order) and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382859 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a1bee282-2775-59f5-a929-9dc5327703bc" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Quadratic Function Over the 2D-Space to the Canonical Form Using Orthogonal Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "380a56bf-13ca-526a-a152-21969eeed029" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382861} {:answer/id #uuid "ea1181ab-306d-55b2-892d-0b7918f50c13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382862} {:answer/id #uuid "1963adc5-9bac-55a5-b8e0-9eea9c095442" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382863} {:answer/id #uuid "d843cfb8-3036-582e-9353-20ddeee40ca4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382864} {:answer/id #uuid "20d0544f-c482-5dd5-98f9-b641127e854b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382865}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea1181ab-306d-55b2-892d-0b7918f50c13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382862} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3fbda42-9fc6-5c6b-8e87-1d3901292df0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382860}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f92c48bb-fec5-5fb3-9d76-72c2d4d25f64" :question/math-academy-id "q-140998" :question/problem "Given the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+9{x}_{2}^{2},$ find its canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables." :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ -3 & 9-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =10,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-10I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{aligned}]-10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-10I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\sqrt{1}0}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ (writing the eigenvalues in descending order) and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162556 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a9a1aa0-8822-5dbd-8534-254ace55c555" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Canonical Form of a Quadratic Function by Finding Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db1dbf4d-28a2-5d5a-87a4-265ea6b7fe91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3y_{1}^{2} + 2y_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160969} {:answer/id #uuid "f3223822-6f0e-5c31-80a6-7a560ccb85bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3y_{1}^{2} - 2y_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160970} {:answer/id #uuid "0c65c312-10f2-5464-b5eb-0a8a5c4a24c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3y_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160971} {:answer/id #uuid "663da8b7-3410-5291-868a-05b65163a3d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2y_{1}^{2} - 3y_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160972} {:answer/id #uuid "7d778a3b-8dc1-53d9-9b9b-c2dcb5d280a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y_{1}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160973}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3223822-6f0e-5c31-80a6-7a560ccb85bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3y_{1}^{2} - 2y_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160970} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4ddf5b3-0488-55e6-bb51-ec7b4aa70d5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160968}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e7899e1b-4eee-518b-aa53-ed72b98bf89e" :question/math-academy-id "q-94446" :question/problem "Using the method of orthogonal transformations, find the canonical form of $Q(x)=-{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -6 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)(\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, writing the eigenvalues in descending order, we obtain the diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe corresponding quadratic form after a change of variables is\n\n$$\n3{y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{2}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162557 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a9a1aa0-8822-5dbd-8534-254ace55c555" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Canonical Form of a Quadratic Function by Finding Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "807ea595-f5a3-5281-a2c3-4ef489f95228" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{1}^{2} - y_{2}^{2} + 2y_{3}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160975} {:answer/id #uuid "f9e9f576-127f-56fa-ae95-3359c5cac537" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4y_{1}^{2} - 2y_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160976} {:answer/id #uuid "2d71aad6-66bd-5ff8-90f1-cd7b1255a767" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4y_{1}^{2} - y_{2}^{2} - 2y_{3}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160977} {:answer/id #uuid "5557225a-dee8-5411-bf7e-482a3aa01bbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-y_{1}^{2} - 2y_{2}^{2} - 2y_{3}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160978} {:answer/id #uuid "6ac6eceb-17ce-55e9-bcf7-bc9866d04ac1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y_{1}^{2} + y_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160979}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d71aad6-66bd-5ff8-90f1-cd7b1255a767" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4y_{1}^{2} - y_{2}^{2} - 2y_{3}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160977} :answer-field/id #uuid "0785eadd-8f77-5860-b9ab-0d2b5b750918" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160974}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "72599bcc-35e4-5761-98ef-23a9eae51976" :question/math-academy-id "q-94449" :question/problem "Using the method of orthogonal transformations, find the canonical form of $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}-{x}_{3}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-9) & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -8) & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )(\\lambda -4)(\\lambda +2) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =4,\\,-1,\\,-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, writing the eigenvalues in descending order, we obtain the diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe corresponding quadratic form after a change of variables is\n\n$$\n4{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}-2{y}_{3}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162559 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a07cbbfc-0f61-55e8-afe9-b675dc74f24e" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Quadratic Function Over the 3D-Space to the Canonical Form Using Orthogonal Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93afd1e7-589c-572d-81e8-cd95c2c7e1a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160987} {:answer/id #uuid "b5ead3be-52cb-51bc-a4ec-b140d0258480" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160988} {:answer/id #uuid "ca5ddbfb-38cf-5614-b107-bae7c3df3a66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160989} {:answer/id #uuid "2ce52d2a-928e-513a-97af-4fe0bcce819b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160990} {:answer/id #uuid "5809b31c-60e6-5689-a138-5e6ee5628d38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160991}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5ead3be-52cb-51bc-a4ec-b140d0258480" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160988} :answer-field/id #uuid "a39de5df-822b-5722-89ab-814c839cfed2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160986}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7f0148f4-83df-5e99-8325-e264016c452b" :question/math-academy-id "q-94461" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices given above.\nFind an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables that reduces the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax$ to the canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy.$" :question/worked-solution "Every quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=10,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=5,\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=3.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-10I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]-10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-10I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-3I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{3}‖=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382866 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a9a1aa0-8822-5dbd-8534-254ace55c555" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Canonical Form of a Quadratic Function by Finding Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c997789-8859-5c29-b117-e43de29abd90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382868} {:answer/id #uuid "98c72e1c-d475-5754-a14d-8e4d3a42992d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{1}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382869} {:answer/id #uuid "68231d48-b7a9-50ef-846e-3b8dd02c4584" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{y}_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382870} {:answer/id #uuid "ee53b4bd-9aa6-5905-9afa-6591f3285b02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382871} {:answer/id #uuid "0ad3475c-e37c-589a-8d5f-cd8a5eddc2db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382872}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee53b4bd-9aa6-5905-9afa-6591f3285b02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382871} :answer-field/id #uuid "d37f18d4-2a54-5979-a83d-6bdce01512fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382867}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e5eb3325-8950-5c51-91d6-e1c0a28e3d5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-94898" :question/problem "Using the method of orthogonal transformations, find the canonical form of $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, writing the eigenvalues in descending order, we obtain the diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe corresponding quadratic form after a change of variables is\n\n$$\n3{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382873 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a9a1aa0-8822-5dbd-8534-254ace55c555" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Canonical Form of a Quadratic Function by Finding Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76027cfb-47bb-5ada-915d-93c9459dd680" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382875} {:answer/id #uuid "bee9c0a5-6d6b-5ffc-a22a-b6a09d9f409b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}-{y}_{3}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382876} {:answer/id #uuid "5e6304e9-50e8-53c6-90cf-6358439923bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}-{y}_{3}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382877} {:answer/id #uuid "1c0199c1-9fbc-58be-b632-9c2a35974053" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}+{y}_{3}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382878} {:answer/id #uuid "2864d932-5de9-50cd-917e-8b1c136da86f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{3}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382879}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bee9c0a5-6d6b-5ffc-a22a-b6a09d9f409b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}-{y}_{3}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382876} :answer-field/id #uuid "57dc18fa-db4b-51f7-bd70-5777ce626d72" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382874}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "07181131-caae-520e-9ffd-8a571dcaad72" :question/math-academy-id "q-94908" :question/problem "Using the method of orthogonal transformations, find the canonical form of $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-4) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,1,\\,-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, writing the eigenvalues in descending order, we obtain the diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe corresponding quadratic form after a change of variables is\n\n$$\n3{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}-{y}_{3}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382880 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a1bee282-2775-59f5-a929-9dc5327703bc" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Quadratic Function Over the 2D-Space to the Canonical Form Using Orthogonal Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb2efc1f-3c2b-5d96-a033-376c4d15b16a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382882} {:answer/id #uuid "2dad5629-755a-5608-86ca-69bba62e6479" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382883} {:answer/id #uuid "9ac40c2e-e06c-56ee-957d-02e9c30c340c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382884} {:answer/id #uuid "7c69188d-583c-5316-ab22-f684a5a9f558" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382885} {:answer/id #uuid "0b3aad92-db58-5c42-8e64-68a3bdb45c21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382886}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2dad5629-755a-5608-86ca-69bba62e6479" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382883} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f33e86c-8e79-54ce-a7ef-f76561e538f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382881}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0d232faf-2de0-5a39-91fc-eea4fa2a2bb3" :question/math-academy-id "q-95069" :question/problem "Given the quadratic form $Q(x)=-3{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2},$ find its canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables." :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-3-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +8 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-2,\\,-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-2)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-4)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}]-(-4)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-4)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ (writing the eigenvalues in descending order) and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382887 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a1bee282-2775-59f5-a929-9dc5327703bc" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Quadratic Function Over the 2D-Space to the Canonical Form Using Orthogonal Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e0ee0ff-e81d-53a1-ba15-5356231bc4f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382889} {:answer/id #uuid "145e589c-06c9-5aea-92dd-9b741dfb469b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382890} {:answer/id #uuid "f8c8af81-1533-5a16-a3af-ae8125c77e15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382891} {:answer/id #uuid "72585252-4b88-568a-a0b4-78d669818c70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382892} {:answer/id #uuid "153c2b85-c4c8-5f6a-b238-3345ba47e53e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382893}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "153c2b85-c4c8-5f6a-b238-3345ba47e53e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382893} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c79817a-42bd-537a-b141-1650ecaa82f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382888}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "51bd93f1-00c1-504d-a9b3-5e4ed39fa2fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-95071" :question/problem "Given the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+12{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-4{x}_{2}^{2},$ find its canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables." :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -40 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =5,\\,-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{13},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-8)I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{aligned}]-(-8)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-8)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{13},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ (writing the eigenvalues in descending order) and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382894 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a1bee282-2775-59f5-a929-9dc5327703bc" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Quadratic Function Over the 2D-Space to the Canonical Form Using Orthogonal Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f51d6454-5243-5ffe-9a8d-269bfe8bbb6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382896} {:answer/id #uuid "514dd2eb-445e-5d2d-98d1-043c886394e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382897} {:answer/id #uuid "826ebd89-e64c-5d6b-8d0b-3eec48c867bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -15\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382898} {:answer/id #uuid "02d60d25-29bc-5e0f-9a69-4a7b17099c96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -15\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382899} {:answer/id #uuid "a289ee0c-7af9-5c41-b053-4a0e18cfb470" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382900}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "514dd2eb-445e-5d2d-98d1-043c886394e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382897} :answer-field/id #uuid "41be5ff6-7520-52cd-a14e-5b648f456578" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382895}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "84da4bfe-23c6-56a5-9883-ca3f2fee6613" :question/math-academy-id "q-95092" :question/problem "Given the quadratic form $Q(x)=14{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2},$ find its canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables." :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}14 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}14-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-20\\lambda +75 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =15,\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=15,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-15I & =[\\begin{aligned}14 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{aligned}]-15[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-15I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}14 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ (writing the eigenvalues in descending order) and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382901 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a07cbbfc-0f61-55e8-afe9-b675dc74f24e" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Quadratic Function Over the 3D-Space to the Canonical Form Using Orthogonal Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1fde2c3-9b6c-5ea9-aa9e-8df95de6194a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382903} {:answer/id #uuid "d2ec7036-d2a0-5ff9-ae60-c885663ae14b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382904} {:answer/id #uuid "1c78ed55-6ae2-52ba-ab00-48d5ca9c33ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382905} {:answer/id #uuid "9dac2610-f8fd-55b0-80c9-5b9dcdc3e80b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382906} {:answer/id #uuid "70e57bfc-9186-5950-8e9b-8ac2be8429fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382907}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70e57bfc-9186-5950-8e9b-8ac2be8429fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382907} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8d36d72-b7cb-5e79-a3d3-73d9b22c696d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382902}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f3b5dfdd-d519-5388-b5ca-0b0271c9801c" :question/math-academy-id "q-95100" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices given above.\nFind an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables that reduces the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax$ to the canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy.$" :question/worked-solution "Every quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=0,\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=-2.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-2)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{3}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382908 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a07cbbfc-0f61-55e8-afe9-b675dc74f24e" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Quadratic Function Over the 3D-Space to the Canonical Form Using Orthogonal Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75fb51e7-f673-59b1-bf06-c48b7d024a34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382910} {:answer/id #uuid "19f09a3a-677b-5a4b-811b-ad1df28ea253" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382911} {:answer/id #uuid "e8bfcef9-7f41-5da3-8bbe-1f4b023b2632" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382912} {:answer/id #uuid "d5746d10-e2f9-53ec-8b2c-bc1c73ffbb2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382913} {:answer/id #uuid "01caabcc-bdab-5558-8e12-fe162041ffa7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382914}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8bfcef9-7f41-5da3-8bbe-1f4b023b2632" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382912} :answer-field/id #uuid "dade6dfb-7edd-58a7-aceb-d64bd8b0da8e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382909}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1cb2c63c-3cd4-5b0f-9b7a-5452eee71130" :question/math-academy-id "q-95101" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices given above.\nFind an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables that reduces the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax$ to the canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy.$" :question/worked-solution "Every quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=-3.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-2)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-3)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]-(-3)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-3)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{3}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382915 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a07cbbfc-0f61-55e8-afe9-b675dc74f24e" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Quadratic Function Over the 3D-Space to the Canonical Form Using Orthogonal Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28990e21-35a7-5d8c-98fb-1909f0c6f409" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382917} {:answer/id #uuid "ca07d2d5-076e-5370-8d1b-ed7bc0844c42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382918} {:answer/id #uuid "75494d25-e44b-5fd0-a2a0-4fe0322b0959" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382919} {:answer/id #uuid "63f5980e-4efb-5d6a-a8a6-669efa4942c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382920} {:answer/id #uuid "771b7a96-6238-5272-ae1f-eb1cda4d0683" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382921}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75494d25-e44b-5fd0-a2a0-4fe0322b0959" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382919} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8dfba0b-88de-5d84-aea5-a8b8f158cbbb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382916}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "358ee2ec-538a-5c65-b3c6-9c060f331c36" :question/math-academy-id "q-95102" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices given above.\nFind an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables that reduces the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax$ to the canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy.$" :question/worked-solution "Every quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-3I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]-(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{3}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382922 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a07cbbfc-0f61-55e8-afe9-b675dc74f24e" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Quadratic Function Over the 3D-Space to the Canonical Form Using Orthogonal Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a452dd8-1550-5791-af0e-2127a6736717" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382924} {:answer/id #uuid "e4f79a49-d35b-5f28-98ba-45540a6debe6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382925} {:answer/id #uuid "ce621e29-9ed2-5486-92db-82f88cfa4755" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382926} {:answer/id #uuid "461cb8cf-68ed-5217-b4e2-6fe7fb1166d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382927} {:answer/id #uuid "45f2ac83-3133-5a86-8ec9-4a30719ff6fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382928}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce621e29-9ed2-5486-92db-82f88cfa4755" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382926} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a1f4916-89aa-5a99-b8ae-6dc757f93c9c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382923}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fb0b43c2-0c8a-5ba6-b111-27a3f0d7a440" :question/math-academy-id "q-95103" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices given above.\nFind an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables that reduces the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax$ to the canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy.$" :question/worked-solution "Every quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=10,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=0.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-10I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]-10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-10I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{3}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}]}
